    {
      "id": "toolu_01CAY39H1EvNiH1xJqNGQjJe",
      "sequence": 137,
      "name": "mcp__capy__capy_index",
      "input": {
        "source": "kk:review-findings",
        "content": "Pattern: \"Simplifying\" a conditional compare-and-delete into an unconditional delete removes an ownership guard and causes stale-eviction. Example: cleanup_setup popped store.active[workspace_id] only when the active association's setup_id matched the setup being cleaned up (`if association and association[\"setup_id\"] == setup_id`). Collapsing it to an unconditional `store.active.pop(workspace_id, None)` lets a late/out-of-order cleanup of a failed setup evict a DIFFERENT, newer setup that already completed for the same key \u2014 silently destroying its live association. Deterministic even in serialized (non-threaded) code when operations are queued/reordered. Red flag: a guarded pop/delete keyed by an identity/owner field being reduced to an unconditional one during a \"simplification\" or \"cleanup\" diff. Check-then-act guards keyed on an owner id are usually intentional (last-writer-wins protection); verify against the documented lifecycle before removing. Existing tests may still pass because they only cover the self-owned path, not the superseded-owner path."
      },
      "result_sequence": 138,
      "is_error": false
